---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description="How ChronoRoute labels every number as REAL, DERIVED, MODELLED or UNKNOWN, where the data comes from, what is modelled, the limitations, related work and what is new."
>
  <header class="page-hero container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p>
    <h1>Where every number comes from</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      This project studies a routing protocol on the geometry of a real constellation, but much of what happens
      inside a real satellite network is not public. So every number and every visual on this site says where it
      comes from, and we do not show numbers we do not have.
    </p>
    <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#labels">Labels</a></li>
        <li><a href="#inventory">What is real</a></li>
        <li><a href="#orbits">Orbit data</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sources">Sources</a></li>
        <li><a href="#limitations">Limitations</a></li>
        <li><a href="#related">Related work</a></li>
        <li><a href="#results">Results</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <!-- OWNER: content -->
  <section class="section" id="labels" aria-labelledby="labels-title">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section__head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Provenance</p>
        <h2 id="labels-title">The four labels</h2>
        <p>Each label has its own colour and its own shape, so it can be read without colour.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="stack-table">
          <caption>Provenance labels used on this site</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Label</th>
              <th scope="col">Meaning</th>
              <th scope="col">Example in this project</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              LABELS.map((label) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row"><LabelChip kind={label.kind} /></th>
                  <td>{label.definition}</td>
                  <td class="muted">{label.example}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="prose rules">
        <h3>Rules we follow</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            A value is only as strong as its weakest input. A route is computed by our code, so the project’s shared
            label table calls it DERIVED, but it runs over MODELLED laser links: it is always shown together with the
            MODELLED label and called hypothetical.
          </li>
          <li>
            The labels come from one table in the code (<code>sim/chronoroute/labels.py</code>), used by every data
            file the project writes; this site’s copy is checked against it on every build.
          </li>
          <li>We do not show placeholder or invented numbers. Where data does not exist yet, the page says so.</li>
          <li>
            Starlink’s real routing is UNKNOWN. Starlink does not use T-CRH; we use its public orbits only as a
            realistic test geometry.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- OWNER: content -->
  <section class="section" id="inventory" aria-labelledby="inventory-title">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section__head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Inventory</p>
        <h2 id="inventory-title">What is real, derived, modelled and unknown</h2>
        <p>Every input of the experiments, with its label and where it comes from.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="stack-table inventory">
          <caption>Inputs and their provenance</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">What</th>
              <th scope="col">Label</th>
              <th scope="col">Where it comes from</th>
              <th scope="col">Notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              inventory.map((row) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">
                    {row.what}
                    {row.planned && <span class="planned-tag">Planned, not built yet</span>}
                  </th>
                  <td class="inventory__label">
                    {row.labelNote ? <LabelChip kind={row.label} note={row.labelNote} /> : <LabelChip kind={row.label} />}
                  </td>
                  <td>{row.how}</td>
                  <td class="muted">{row.note}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- OWNER: content -->
  <section class="section" id="orbits" aria-labelledby="orbit-title">
    <div class="container split">
      <div class="section__head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Orbit data</p>
        <h2 id="orbit-title">From public elements to positions</h2>
        <div class="chips">
          <LabelChip kind="real" note="elements" />
          <LabelChip kind="real" note="positions (SGP4)" />
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">
        <ol class="prose pipeline">
          <li>
            We download CelesTrak’s supplemental GP (SupGP) elements for Starlink. CelesTrak derives them from
            ephemerides published by SpaceX. The download time is taken from the HTTP Date header, and the raw file
            is kept with its sha256 hash.
          </li>
          <li>
            Element sets whose epoch is more than three days older than the download are left out. Every remaining
            set must initialise and propagate with SGP4 at the download time; any that fail are left out too. The
            snapshot’s manifest records how many were left out and why.
          </li>
          <li>
            SGP4 propagates each element set to the time you are looking at. Its error grows with the time since
            each element set’s epoch, so the site always shows how old the snapshot is.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <DataBadge />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- OWNER: content -->
  <section class="section" id="sources" aria-labelledby="sources-title">
    <div class="container split">
      <div class="section__head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Sources</p>
        <h2 id="sources-title">Data, standards and prior work</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="sources-wrap">
        <h3 class="sources__title">Data and standards</h3>
        <ul class="sources">
          {
            sources.map((s) => (
              <li>
                <a href={s.href} rel="noopener">{s.title}</a>
                <p>{s.detail}</p>
                {s.code && (
                  <p class="sources__code">
                    {s.code.label}: <code>{s.code.text}</code>
                  </p>
                )}
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
        <h3 class="sources__title" id="prior-work">Prior work</h3>
        <ul class="sources">
          {
            priorWork.map((s) => (
              <li>
                <a href={s.href} rel="noopener">{s.title}</a>
                <p>{s.detail}</p>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- OWNER: content -->
  <section class="section" id="limitations" aria-labelledby="limitations-title">
    <div class="container split">
      <div class="section__head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Limitations</p>
        <h2 id="limitations-title">What this work does not show</h2>
      </div>
      <ul class="prose limits">
        <li>
          <strong>It is a simulation.</strong> Results will come from our Python simulator and ns-3 module, on
          modelled links, traffic and failures. Nothing here is measured on Starlink.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Orbits are a snapshot.</strong> SGP4 positions get less accurate as the element sets age.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Clocks must agree.</strong> The check assumes every node knows GPS time. A node whose clock is off
          by some amount shifts its verdicts by the same amount.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The alarm comes after the fact.</strong> The node at the far end of a slow link notices; the
          packet has already crossed that link.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Resolution is one unit.</strong> Lateness shorter than the deadline unit, or hidden by the slack
          S, is not seen. Slack trades missed problems against false alarms, which is exactly what the experiments
          will measure.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A hop budget is at most 255 units</strong> (25.5 ms at the provisional default unit of 0.1 ms).
          A longer budget is clamped, the SAT flag is set, and that deadline is then earlier than its budget.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Three header fields change in flight</strong> (Segments Left, Flags, Dev Hop): two more mutable
          fields than RFC 9631 allows, since only Segments Left changes in a CRH. This matters for IPsec AH.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Experimental and closed-domain.</strong> T-CRH uses an experimental routing type (253) and is
          meant for a single operator’s network, not the public Internet.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The fallback can fail.</strong> Greedy geographic forwarding can reach a node with no neighbour
          closer to the destination, so falling back does not guarantee delivery.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- OWNER: content -->
  <section class="section" id="related" aria-labelledby="related-title">
    <div class="container split">
      <div class="section__head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Related work</p>
        <h2 id="related-title">What exists, and what is new</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          <strong>Compact source routing.</strong> SRv6 (RFC 8754) carries a full 128-bit address per segment;
          compressed SRv6 (RFC 9800) and the Compact Routing Header (RFC 9631) make the list much smaller. T-CRH
          builds directly on CRH-16.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>LEO network simulation.</strong> Hypatia (Kassing et al., IMC 2020) simulates LEO satellite
          networks at packet level. Its default laser-link layout, Grid-4 (+Grid), is the link model this project
          uses (see the <a href="#inventory">inventory</a>).
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Per-hop timing in source routes.</strong> Putting timing for each hop into a source-routed packet
          is not new, and ChronoRoute does not claim to be the first to do it. CGREB (Birrane, 2013) carries the
          planned contacts of a route with their time windows, and each node re-checks that they are still
          feasible. SRTSN (Stein, 2021) and the Deterministic Routing Header, called CRH-20 here (Peng and Bonica,
          2024), carry a per-hop deadline or residence time that routers use to schedule their queues; the
          Deterministic Source Route Header (Peng, 2024) and the deadline-based forwarding drafts for DetNet belong
          to the same family. These designs use time as a scheduling input, or check whether link windows still exist.
          T-CRH uses a cumulative arrival deadline as an alarm instead: one comparison per hop, which also catches a
          link that is still up but slow.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Routing in LEO constellations.</strong> STARGLIDER (Vissicchio and Handley, 2024) has the same
          architecture: routes are computed on the ground, the path travels in the packet, and satellites keep no
          routing state. Its header is much smaller than T-CRH’s, and it reacts to detected link failures rather
          than to timing. GCR (Hartig et al., LEO-NET 2024) combines segment routing with geographic forwarding.
          Greedy geographic forwarding itself, as in GPSR, is prior art: in ChronoRoute it is a safety net, not a
          contribution. Full references are in the <a href="#prior-work">source list</a>.
        </p>
        <figure class="novelty">
          <figcaption>What is new</figcaption>
          <blockquote>
            <p>
              To our knowledge, no earlier design uses cumulative per-hop arrival deadlines in an IPv6 routing header
              as a built-in alarm: with one comparison, each satellite on the path can tell that reality has drifted
              from the predicted LEO schedule, including links that are up but slow. The geographic forwarding that
              follows an alarm is not new. The experiments will measure how well the alarm works (catch rate against
              false alarms) on real Starlink orbit data.
            </p>
          </blockquote>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- OWNER: content -->
  <section class="section" id="results" aria-labelledby="results-title">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section__head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Results</p>
        <h2 id="results-title">Experiment results</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose results-now">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Pre-registered experiments.</strong> Six experiments (E0, E1, E2, E4, E5, E16) were registered
            before they ran and analysed with the registered rules. Each has its own page on the
            <a href={pageHref('experiments')}>experiments page</a> with the question, the hypothesis, the verdict,
            charts with confidence intervals, the data and its run manifest (schema
            <code>chronoroute.manifest/1</code>, in <code>spec/schemas/</code>): the code commit, the configuration
            and its hash, the random seeds, the orbit snapshot, library versions and the SHA-256 hash of every output
            file.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Corrected semester results.</strong> The earlier semester code had known bugs. Its experiments have
            been re-run on the bug-fixed code, each with a provenance manifest, in
            <a href={`${REPO_URL}/tree/main/results/legacy-v0.9`} rel="noopener"><code>results/legacy-v0.9</code></a>.
